Based on the provided journal issue, I will summarize the first article in the "Pedagogy and Psychology for Education" section, which is titled "Problems of emergency distance learning: The point of view of education stakeholders" by Kostina, Kalabina, Nikolaeva, and Dunaevskaya.

---

The article examines the problems that arose during the emergency transition to distance learning, as experienced by different stakeholders in the educational process: schoolchildren, undergraduate and postgraduate students, teachers, and parents. The study, which surveyed 664 participants, found that while students generally had a positive attitude towards distance learning, the transition created significant psychological problems for parents and teachers.

For schoolchildren, the main issues were technical. A third of them relied on smartphones or tablets, and 36% had to share a device with a family member. Despite these challenges, students reported a positive experience with remote learning. The most significant problems were observed among the adult participants. Parents, who were suddenly expected to take on the role of teacher, reported that the process was extremely time-consuming and described symptoms of burnout. Teachers faced a similar crisis, describing the experience as a "double burden" because they were simultaneously performing their professional duties online while also trying to support their own children's education at home. This role conflict and the lack of time for personal recovery were key factors in the reported stress and burnout.

The study identified four main types of problems: technical, interpersonal, individual, and existential. The researchers concluded that the negative experiences of adults were likely not caused by distance learning itself, but rather by the fact that the crisis revealed pre-existing, unresolved family issues.